lly  have to resort to  you know high sensitivity cameras more  expensive cameras and here just for  presenting on the tip  of this of the snout of this short  embryo  you can see that you can get you know  fairly good  resolution that with optical tomography  these are images of optical tomography  and they're fully isometric no matter  which angle you look from which plane  you're trying to  re-slice your sample you get always the  same kind of detail  for more detail we can resort to spim  light sheets  illumination and then you get even more  detail and that's what we're working on  the challenge then is to actually  combine the two images  and keep them on the on the same data  set so to speak  which you can still do using fiji these  days and  and big that the viewer for example or  other software solutions  so that's pretty much what we've been  working on i would just like to end by  highlighting the strengths and the  limitations of optical tomography for  to try to convince you whether you  should or you should not get into it  um as i said it's very easy and cheap to  build and implement  there are multiple open source solutions  out there the one that we developed  called opent  is just one of them it's much easier to  operate and maintain than a light sheet  microscope or even an open spin  or a micro spin  even though these have different  applications once again i don't want to  portray optical tomography as  a competitive as a competitor but if  if you're venturing for the first time  into building something in your lab  and and if all you need is you know low  magnification  and not high resolution cellulose of  cellular level  information if all you want is to  reconstruct organs in 3d  on a large embryo maybe optical  tomography is just enough for you  and it's much easier to build as i said  and operate  it doesn't require lasers or complex  optics or even sensitive cameras  um it has this added value of allowing  you to do 3d imaging using bright fields  so you don't have to do staining which  can be fairly challenging  on very large embryos or even producing  transgenics  frozen proteins just out of fluorescence  is another and if you don't want to use  other fluorescence even bright field is  enough  in some cases bright field might  actually be more powerful than doing  fluorescence because  if you're doing red shifted illumination  you can actually image deeper and see  details deeper  into uh into the samples um i've also  mentioned this  great advantage is the fact that you  have inherently isometric  reconstructions  no matter which angle you choose to see  the slices from  um you will get the same quality which  is not true of  you know even light cheap microscopy  unless you do multi-view reconstruction  and multi-viewer construction is  cumbersome  it takes quite a while it's data hungry  it requires very fast computers  and very often you don't you don't get  real isometric reconstruction from the  samples it's fairly complex to do that  um so the one thing that is typically  seen as a disadvantage of optical  tomography it's the fact that you don't  get instant gratification you don't get  immediate  optical slices if you consider that you  have to do multiview reconstruction with  logic microscopy through these  mesoscopic samples  so that also is computation that also  requires time  and so in in a way the fact that you get  immediately a symmetric reconstructions  may can actually also be seen as a  strength of optical tomography you're  done you reconstruct but you're done  no not everything is  easy obviously there are some  limitations the  fact that you need to see through  require that you have to kill things and  make them transparent  um and because you have to see through  the whole sample  typically you have to resort to low  numerical aperture objectives low  magnifications which don't give you know  the high  cellular and subcellular resolution  which you might or might not require  it also severely limits your ability to  capture photons  so uh you very often resort to long  exposures and you have sort of bleaching  and that is a problem that you need to  address  occasionally with optical tomography now  if you don't use it correctly  it does generate lots of artifacts and  you have to do  fairly familiar with optical  microscopy sorry with image processing  techniques  and applying image processing techniques  to avoid some of these artifacts but  um bear in mind that's reality also if  you're doing  mesoscopic imaging with spin or even  convocal or to photon imaging  now the the the one thing that takes  people off from  trying to do optical tomography is the  fact that  it requires this filtered back  projection reconstruction to obtain the  optical slices  um but you know we've been trying to  convince people this is actually not  that  difficult i'm a biology researcher i  didn't have computer training computer  science training  and i was able to figure this out with  the help of colleagues so if you  if you do want to venture into this you  can start by searching our publication  in 2013  there's also now another open source  project  um called optij which is um fairly easy  to use as well if you want to implement  and learn how to reconstruct things  completely with like with the sorry  optical tomography  um and there's a new chapter coming out  we just had a new chapter that's coming  out on a book  very soon so contact us if you want more  information this chapter actually  describes every step and it provides you  with places where you can get the  software  to make these three-dimensional  reconstructions so i hope i was able to  convince you that optical tomography is  interesting enough that  it might be worth it to look into and i  just want to finish  by thanking um the funding that made  this process  this project possible and then also  nikon who  gave us a price for this beautiful image  of the coil emitter that  you saw in the beginning of the of the  talk and and also  many of the collaborators who have given  us samples and provided us with  challenges  for these images and and some of the  people i've had the privilege to work  with  in this project and other projects  [Music]  most of them have left the institute but  i've been lucky enough that  every time someone goes away i get  another  chance to work with another person and  now we have alex and very soon we're  going to have another  person working on this project and also  i would like to thank the rest of the  team for the support and the discussions  throughout these years  and again thank you all for taking the  time to watch this webinar  stay safe
 

